# November 16, 2026: ruins in the woods, reached by trail on public or preserve land, across the Northeast.
# Facts come from the linked sources only: Wikipedia articles read through tools/wiki_raw.py, plus the Forest Society,
# Scenic Hudson, Vermont State Parks, the Town of Hawley and the Historical Marker Database where linked.
# Where a source is silent the text says so and conf is set; nothing here is filled in from memory.
# Pins come from the article's coordinates or a named OpenStreetMap feature (looked up through Nominatim), as each
# pin note says. Each was looked at once on an aerial (research/pincheck_woods_*.jpg). Most sit under tree cover,
# where an aerial cannot confirm a ruin, so the notes say what was and was not visible. No pin was moved by eye.
# Trailhead coordinates (th) were not sourced for this batch; the access note names the road or lot instead.
from data_b import N
from data_ma import W
BATCH={'key':'woods','label':'Ruins in the woods','added':'2026-11-16'}
FS=['Forest Society: Madame Sherri Forest','https://forestsociety.org/madamesherri']
PLACES=[
# ---- New York: Hudson Highlands and Shawangunks ----
N('doodletown','Doodletown','Stony Point','midh','town','ruins',41.29664,-73.99475,'site','public','hike','cemeteries','1762–1965',
  'A hamlet in a valley behind Bear Mountain. Ithiel June bought 72 acres here in 1762, and by the 1920s there were about 70 homes, a school, a church, a few businesses and two cemeteries. The Palisades Interstate Park Commission bought the place up from about 1920; the last holdouts lost their land by eminent domain by 1965, and most buildings were torn down or moved by the late 1960s. The stone schoolhouse was kept as a hikers\' shelter until vandalism led the commission to pull it down in 1980.',
  'Foundations, walls and staircases along the old roads, and the hamlet\'s cemeteries. The remains of small iron mines are nearby.',
  'A whole village bought out for a park, with its streets still walkable.',
  'Bear Mountain State Park. Reached on foot by trails and the former roads; there is no driving in.',[W('Doodletown, New York')]),
N('dunderberg-spiral-railway','Dunderberg Spiral Railway','Stony Point','midh','rail','traces',41.28658,-73.97088,'site','public','hike','inclines','1889–1891',
  'In 1889 a company formed to build a hotel on top of Dunderberg Mountain, with a steam railway to carry tourists up to it. The money ran out in 1891 and the line was never finished. Why it failed is not clear.',
  'Graded stretches of the unfinished line and some ruins of the construction, on the mountainside above Jones Point. The source does not list the individual features.',
  'A mountain railway that was graded and never ran a train.',
  'Bear Mountain State Park. The graded sections are reached from the Ramapo-Dunderberg and Timp-Torne trails.',[W('Dunderberg Mountain')],conf='mixed'),
N('fort-montgomery-highlands','Fort Montgomery','Highlands','midh','fort','ruins',41.32389,-73.98694,'site','public','walk','forts','1776–1777',
  'The Continental Army began this fort in March 1776 on a cliff 100 feet above the Hudson, at the mouth of Popolopen Creek, with a river battery, landward redoubts and a chain and boom across the river. On October 6, 1777 about 2,100 Loyalist, Hessian and British troops attacked it and the smaller Fort Clinton across the creek. Both fell that day; the British burned them and tore down the stone buildings.',
  'The ruins of the fort, on a system of trails with interpretive signs. A visitor center opened in October 2006 and holds finds from the site and a model of the fort.',
  '','Fort Montgomery State Historic Site, owned and run by New York State. A National Historic Landmark.',[W('Fort Montgomery (Hudson River)')]),
N('cornish-estate-northgate','Cornish Estate (Northgate)','Philipstown','midh','landmark','ruins',41.4377,-73.97015,'exact','public','walk','burned','c. 1910–1958',
  'A stone and brick mansion on the side of Bull Hill, built over about a decade by the diamond merchant Sigmund Stern. Edward Cornish and his wife Selina bought the 650 acres in 1917 and called it Northgate. In the 1930s Cornish offered the land to the state, which turned it down. A fire in 1958 destroyed most of the house, and the land joined Hudson Highlands State Park in the late 1960s.',
  'The roofless stone and brick shell of the house above the river, and a barn and garage complex nearby. Signs installed by the Northgate Project explain the ruins.',
  'A burned mansion you can walk through, ten minutes from a trailhead.',
  'Hudson Highlands State Park Preserve. An easy 1.8-mile round trip up the estate\'s old driveway from the state park lot on Route 9D north of Cold Spring, across the road from Little Stony Point.',
  [['Scenic Hudson: Cornish Estate Ruins','https://www.scenichudson.org/explore-the-valley/outdoor-adventures/adventure/cornish-estate-ruins/'],['Hudson Valley Ruins: Northgate','https://www.hudsonvalleyruins.org/yasinsac/cornish/cornish1.html'],['NBC New York on the Northgate Project','https://www.nbcnewyork.com/entertainment/travel/abandoned-new-york-discover-the-hidden-past-behind-the-cornish-estate-ruins/3203462/']]),
N('cedar-glen-franny-reese','Cedar Glen ruins, Franny Reese State Park','Lloyd','midh','landmark','ruins',41.7041,-73.9566,'area','public','walk','','mid-1800s',
  'Cedar Glen was the estate of Charles H. Roberts, a pioneer of painless dentistry, built in the mid-19th century on the bluff across the river from Poughkeepsie. Scenic Hudson bought the land in 2003 and it opened as a state park in 2009.',
  'The ruins of the 19th-century mansion, beside old carriage roads that are now the park\'s trails.',
  '','Franny Reese State Park. The state owns it and Scenic Hudson manages it.',[W('Franny Reese State Park')]),
N('trapps-mountain-hamlet','Trapps Mountain Hamlet','Gardiner','midh','town','ruins',41.73421,-74.20887,'exact','public','walk','','to 1956',
  'A scattered hamlet of subsistence farmers on the Shawangunk Ridge. Its people also shaved barrel hoops, peeled bark for tanneries, cut millstones and burned charcoal. The town gave up part of Van Leuven Road about 1907, and the last resident died in 1956.',
  'Foundations of most of the buildings in regrown forest; six buildings still stand. The pin is the Van Leuven cabin, which the Mohonk Preserve restored and opened as a historic structure.',
  'One of the few subsistence hill communities in the East that left anything behind.',
  'Mostly Mohonk Preserve and Minnewaska State Park Preserve land, reached by their trails and carriage roads. A few lots are private. The USGS public-land map marks the Mohonk Preserve as restricted by permit, fee or season, so check its day-use rules before you go.',[W('Trapps Mountain Hamlet Historic District')]),
# ---- New York: Catskills and Adirondacks ----
N('coykendall-lodge','Coykendall Lodge','Hardenburgh','cat','landmark','ruins',42.05006,-74.68147,'exact','public','walk','','1899',
  'A large half-timbered lodge built in 1899 on the shore of Alder Lake. It was listed on the National Register in 2002 and is now a ruin. The source does not say when or why it came down.',
  'The stone foundations and cobblestone walls, by the lake.',
  '','Balsam Lake Mountain Wild Forest, in the Catskill Forest Preserve.',[W('Coykendall Lodge')],conf='mixed'),
N('camp-santanoni','Camp Santanoni','Newcomb','adk','landmark','standing',44.01279,-74.13107,'exact','public','hike','','1892–1971',
  'The Albany banker Robert C. Pruyn built this great camp on about 12,900 acres; the main camp went up in 1892–93 and the gate lodge in 1905. His heirs sold to the Melvin family in 1953. In 1971 an eight-year-old grandson, Douglas Legg, vanished in the woods here and was never found, and the family sold the whole preserve, which passed to the state. For two decades the buildings were barely maintained, until a preservation group persuaded the state to keep them.',
  'Three groups of buildings on one road: the gate lodge at the edge of Newcomb, the farm a mile in, and the main camp on Newcomb Lake, 4.7 miles from the gate lodge.',
  'A great camp you can only reach under your own power.',
  'State Forest Preserve land, the Camp Santanoni Historic Area, a National Historic Landmark. No cars: walk, bike, ride or ski the old road from the gate lodge.',[W('Santanoni Preserve')]),
# ---- Massachusetts ----
N('hawley-old-town-common','Hawley Old Town Common','Hawley','pv','town','ruins',42.57963,-72.89175,'site','public','walk','','1771–',
  'The first centre of Hawley, a hill town settled in 1771, with taverns, a stagecoach stop and a meetinghouse built in 1793. The village moved away and the common went back to woods. The town\'s historical groups cleared it and laid out a self-guided trail in 2010.',
  'Cellar holes with signs along a walking path, a kiosk, and a 1935 stone marking the site of the first church. In Dubuque State Forest to the south, a rare fieldstone beehive charcoal kiln stands about 0.8 miles up Kiln Road.',
  'A ghost town common with every cellar hole labelled.',
  'Off East Hawley Road, by the entrance to the Hawley Bog trail. Most of the common belongs to the Town of Hawley; the rest of the historic site is split among Five Colleges, the UMass Foundation, the state forest and a private owner, so stay on the marked path. The USGS public-land map marks the Hawley Bog Preserve next door as restricted access.',
  [['Town of Hawley: Old Town Common tour','https://townofhawley.com/event/ghost-town-tour-of-the-hawley-old-town-common/'],['Historical Marker Database: First Church of Hawley','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=25876'],['Greenfield Recorder on the 2010 trail and kiosk','https://recorder.com/2016/03/03/news-local-hawley-map-kiosk-old-town-common-vandalized/'],['Mass.gov: Kenneth Dubuque Memorial State Forest','https://www.mass.gov/locations/kenneth-dubuque-memorial-state-forest']]),
N('questing-leffingwell','Questing: the Leffingwell settlement','New Marlborough','berk','town','ruins',42.1219,-73.2518,'area','public','hike','','to 1930s',
  'A hilltop where New Marlborough\'s first fort stood and, later, the Leffingwell family\'s farm settlement. The farms were abandoned in the Great Depression. The Trustees of Reservations acquired the land in 1992 and 1996.',
  'Cellar holes and stone walls are all that is left of the farmstead, in woods beyond the reservation\'s meadows.',
  '','Questing, a Trustees of Reservations property. The trailhead is on New Marlborough Hill Road.',[W('Questing (New Marlborough, Massachusetts)')]),
N('noanet-iron-mill','Noanet Woodlands mill site','Dover','bos','industry','traces',42.24694,-71.26889,'area','public','walk','','early 1800s–1876',
  'The Dover Union Iron Company ran a mill on Noanet Brook in the early 19th century and dammed the brook for power. Floods destroyed the dams in 1876. Amelia Peabody had them rebuilt in 1954 after having the mill site investigated.',
  'Three ponds held by the rebuilt dams, and the mill site beside the brook.',
  '','Noanet Woodlands, a Trustees of Reservations property. Parking at 93 Powisset Street and in the town\'s Caryl Park.',[W('Noanet Woodlands')]),
# ---- Vermont ----
N('ricker-basin','Ricker Basin','Waterbury','vtc','town','ruins',44.3899,-72.7672,'area','public','hike','cemeteries','early 1800s–1930s',
  'In the early 1800s settlers cleared farms in Ricker Basin and Cotton Brook, and about 50 families came to live here. The land and the weather were hard and the younger generations left. The flood of November 3 and 4, 1927 drove people onto their roofs, and between 1935 and 1938 the Civilian Conservation Corps and the Army Corps of Engineers built the Waterbury Reservoir below.',
  'Cemeteries, sawmill remains, old town roads, bridges and many cellar holes on the hillside trails. Down by the campground, a few chimneys and concrete foundations are left from a CCC camp that once had more than 80 buildings.',
  'A hill farm community you can walk through one cellar hole at a time.',
  'Little River State Park, 3444 Little River Road, part of Mount Mansfield State Forest. Self-guided history trails start near the campground. The park is staffed from the third weekend in May to the third weekend in October.',
  [['Vermont State Parks: Little River','https://vtstateparks.com/littleriver.html'],W('Little River State Park')]),
N('mount-independence','Mount Independence','Orwell','vtn','fort','traces',43.8258,-73.3817,'site','public','walk','forts','1776–1777',
  'American troops fortified this peninsula on Lake Champlain from July 1776, after the defeat in Canada, and kept building through the spring of 1777. They retreated on July 5 and 6, 1777, and British and Hessian troops held the place until that November.',
  'Trails pass the sites of the blockhouses, the general hospital and the soldiers\' huts. The museum shows timbers from the Great Bridge and a cannon raised from the lake.',
  '','Mount Independence State Historic Site, a National Historic Landmark. The visitor center is open daily from the end of May to mid-October.',[W('Mount Independence (Vermont)')]),
# ---- New Hampshire ----
N('madame-sherri-castle','Madame Sherri\'s castle','Chesterfield','nhs','landmark','ruins',42.86446,-72.51974,'site','public','walk','burned','1920s–1963',
  'Antoinette Sherri, a theatrical costume designer, built a French-style summer house in the woods here and threw parties in it through the 1920s. Her money ran out and the house decayed. It burned in 1963, and she died in 1965. Ann Stokes bought the land soon after and gave the Forest Society an easement in 1976, the forest itself in 1991, and in 2005 the 25.5 acres that hold the ruin.',
  'The stone foundation, chimneys and the outdoor stone staircase. The top arch of the staircase fell on July 11, 2021 after heavy rain, and the ruin was roped off.',
  'A party house in the woods, down to one staircase.',
  'Madame Sherri Forest, 513 acres owned by the Forest Society. Small lot on Gulf Road; the ruin is a short walk up a side trail. Do not climb on the stonework. The USGS public-land map marks this parcel as closed to the public, which disagrees with the owner: the Forest Society publishes the parking and trails, so go by its page.',
  [FS,['Forest Society: the 2021 collapse','https://forestsociety.org/blog-post/stairs-give-way-nature-madame-sherri-forest']]),
N('monson-center','Monson Center','Milford','nhs','town','ruins',42.78611,-71.61944,'site','public','walk','','1737–1770',
  'Six settlers bought this land in 1735 and moved onto it with their families in 1737, making it the earliest inland colonial settlement in what became New Hampshire. Its only public structure was a pound for stray cattle: it never had a school, a meeting hall or a church. The village disbanded in 1770 and was absorbed by the neighbouring towns. Why is not clear. In 1998 a 28-lot subdivision threatened the site and the Forest Society bought it.',
  'Seven home sites along the old village roads, each with a marker about the family: stone foundations and cellar holes of the Gould, Wallingford, Nevins, Bayley and Brown homesteads, with stone walls and open fields. Trail maps are kept in a box on the Gould House.',
  'A colonial village that gave up, kept as it fell.',
  'Owned by the Forest Society. Room for a few cars on Adams Road in front of the gate; the kiosk is a few hundred yards in. Three miles of easy walking on the old roads. No bicycles, fires or camping. Winter access is not guaranteed.',
  [['Forest Society: Monson Center','https://forestsociety.org/property/monson-center'],W('Monson, New Hampshire')]),
N('livermore-nh','Livermore','Livermore','nhw','town','ruins',44.07451,-71.3773,'area','public','walk','','',
  'A logging town on the Sawyer River, set up by Daniel Saunders Jr. and Charles W. Saunders and named for Samuel Livermore. It is now an unincorporated township almost entirely inside the White Mountain National Forest, with a population of two at the 2020 census. The source read does not give its founding or closing dates.',
  'The ruins of the sawmill, found by following the old logging railroad grade into the woods.',
  '','White Mountain National Forest, on the Forest Service\'s Sawyer River Road. Check that the road is open before you drive out.',[W('Livermore, New Hampshire')],conf='mixed'),
N('redstone-quarry','Redstone granite quarry','Conway','nhw','industry','traces',44.01472,-71.10111,'area','public','hike','','',
  'Redstone was built around a quarry of pink "red" granite on the east side of the Saco valley. At its height the quarry ran more than twenty derricks. The closing year is not confirmed: the article gives 1942 in one place and "circa 1950" in another.',
  'The old quarry, at the end of a ridge trail. The source does not describe what machinery is left.',
  '','The Nature Conservancy\'s Green Hills Preserve, created from a 1990 bequest. The pin marks the village of Redstone; the quarry is in the hills behind it.',[W('Redstone, New Hampshire')],conf='mixed'),
N('fort-dearborn-odiorne','Fort Dearborn, Odiorne Point','Rye','nhl','fort','ruins',43.05102,-70.72097,'site','public','walk','forts','1942–1947',
  'In 1942 the government condemned a stretch of expensive oceanfront at Odiorne Point and built Fort Dearborn on it, mainly to mount two 16-inch former Navy guns under concrete and earth casemates. The fort lasted until 1947. In 1961 the state got 137 acres for $91,000, on condition that it be used for recreation; the former owners were given no chance to buy their land back.',
  'The casemated 16-inch battery, now a wooded mound, and other remains of the fort in the park. The pin is Battery Seaman.',
  '','Odiorne Point State Park.',[W('Odiorne Point State Park')]),
# ---- Connecticut ----
N('gay-city','Gay City','Hebron','cte','town','ruins',41.72351,-72.44311,'area','public','walk','burned','1796–1879',
  'Elijah Andrus and his followers settled this hollow on the Blackledge River in 1796. A sawmill and a wool mill followed and the place, then called Factory Hollow, grew to about 25 families, many of them named Gay. The wool mill failed after the War of 1812 and burned in 1830. A paper mill revived the village, then burned in 1879, and that finished it. A descendant sold the land to the state in 1943.',
  'Foundations, stone walls and the mill ditches in deep woods around the old millpond.',
  'A mill village that burned down twice and gave up.',
  'Gay City State Park, off State Route 85.',[W('Gay City State Park')]),
N('barkhamsted-lighthouse','Barkhamsted Lighthouse village','Barkhamsted','ct','town','ruins',41.94225,-73.00643,'area','public','hike','cemeteries','mid-1700s–1860s',
  'A small community founded in the mid-18th century by James Chaugham, a Narragansett from Block Island, and by tradition his wife Molly Barber. Stagecoach drivers on the river road called it the Lighthouse, because its lights told them New Hartford was near. The people left in the 1860s.',
  'Excavations found the foundations of ten buildings, four charcoal kilns, a well and a small cemetery. The stone was quarried on the spot.',
  'A village known to travellers only as a light in the woods.',
  'Peoples State Forest. The site is on the National Register as the Lighthouse Archeological Site. Leave everything as you find it.',[W('Barkhamsted Lighthouse')]),
N('hearthstone-castle','Hearthstone Castle','Danbury','ctsw','landmark','ruins',41.37808,-73.44842,'exact','view','walk','','1895–1899',
  'A stone castle built between 1895 and 1899 for the portrait photographer E. Starr Sanford, from rock quarried on the property and hauled on a small purpose-built railway. Sanford sold it after five years. It was later bought as a wedding present for Irene Parks and named Hearthstone. The whole estate was sold to the city in 1987. It was not kept up and the roof has fallen in.',
  'The roofless stone shell. Look from outside.',
  '','Tarrywile Park, owned by the City of Danbury, which plans to stabilise the shell as an observation deck. Do not go inside.',[W('Hearthstone Castle')]),
# ---- New Jersey ----
N('van-slyke-castle','Van Slyke Castle','Wanaque','njn','landmark','ruins',41.04482,-74.26277,'site','public','hike','burned','early 1900s–1950s',
  'A mansion built to look like a castle by the stockbroker William Porter in the early 1900s, and first called Foxcroft. It was abandoned by the 1950s and then burned by vandals.',
  'The ruined stone walls of the house on the ridge, a regular stop for hikers. OpenStreetMap also maps the ruin of its water tower a short way north on the same trail.',
  '','Ramapo Mountain State Forest, on the Castle Loop Trail.',[W('Ramapo Mountain State Forest')]),
N('feltville','Feltville, the Deserted Village','Berkeley Heights','njn','town','standing',40.67988,-74.38815,'site','public','walk','','1840s–',
  'David Felt, a stationer, built a mill village here in the 1840s. He sold up after fifteen years, the businesses that followed failed, and the place became known as the Deserted Village. Over the years it was a mill town, a farming community and a summer resort.',
  'Eight houses, a church, a carriage house and a general store building in the woods. Three families still live here; the rest is open to walk through.',
  '','Watchung Reservation, a Union County park.',[W('Feltville Historic District')]),
N('long-pond-ironworks','Long Pond Ironworks','West Milford','njn','industry','ruins',41.14525,-74.31177,'site','public','walk','','1766–1882',
  'Peter Hasenclever founded the ironworks in 1766 and brought 500 ironworkers and their families from Germany to build it. A dam at Long Pond, now Greenwood Lake, drove the blast for the furnace and a forge. Iron making stopped in 1882, when the works went bankrupt against newer plants in Pittsburgh.',
  'Furnaces, casting house ruins, ice houses, waterwheels and old stone walls beside the Wanaque River. The old country store is now a museum.',
  '','Long Pond Ironworks State Park. The historic site also runs tours.',[W('Long Pond Ironworks State Park')]),
N('harrisville-nj','Harrisville','Bass River','njs','town','ruins',39.6616,-74.5228,'site','public','walk','burned','c. 1815–1914',
  'A Pine Barrens company town. An earlier works here was converted to a paper mill about 1815, driven by water brought by canal from a dam on the Oswego River. Under the Harris family it had a grist mill, a post office, a company store and free houses for the workers. A fire in 1914 destroyed the whole town.',
  'Decayed ruins of the town, close to where County Route 679 crosses the Oswego River.',
  '','The USGS public-land map puts the pin in Wharton State Forest. The source read does not say whether the ruins are fenced: if they are, stay outside.',[W('Harrisville, New Jersey')],conf='mixed'),
N('pahaquarry-copper-mine','Pahaquarry Copper Mine','Hardwick','njn','industry','ruins',41.03806,-75.0275,'site','public','hike','','to 1911',
  'A copper mine on the Delaware. Legend put Dutch miners here before 1650, but research has refuted that. Companies built an ore mill and tried several times; the last ore was dug in 1911, for three months. From 1925 to 1972 the land was a Boy Scout camp, and the Army Corps of Engineers then bought it for the Tocks Island Dam, which was never built.',
  'Mining ruins along hiking trails, with waterfalls nearby.',
  '','Delaware Water Gap National Recreation Area, National Park Service. Stay out of any mine opening.',[W('Pahaquarry Copper Mine')]),
N('jungle-habitat','Jungle Habitat','West Milford','njn','amuse','traces',41.12991,-74.34095,'site','public','walk','amusements','1972–1976',
  'A Warner Bros. drive-through safari park that opened in the summer of 1972 and drew 500,000 paying visitors by that November. It closed in October 1976. Newspapers reported animal carcasses left to rot, and the decaying buildings stood for years. The state bought the property in 1988 for $1.45 million.',
  'The park\'s paved roads through the woods. The source does not say which buildings or cages survive.',
  'A safari park in the New Jersey woods, four summers long.',
  'Part of Long Pond Ironworks State Park, run from Ringwood State Park. A cycling club cleared it in 2007 and it is used as a trail network.',[W('Warner Bros. Jungle Habitat')],conf='mixed'),
# ---- Pennsylvania ----
N('rausch-gap','Rausch Gap','Cold Spring Township','pac','town','ruins',40.49944,-76.59778,'site','public','hike','cemeteries','1828–1910',
  'Once the largest of the coal towns in St. Anthony\'s Wilderness. It may date from 1828 but grew after 1850, when a coal company built a railroad up the valley and put its shops here. When the railroad moved out the town lost its purpose: fewer than a hundred people were left by 1875.',
  'Stone foundations and a small cemetery in the woods.',
  'A coal and railroad town with only a graveyard left.',
  'The Appalachian Trail\'s Rausch Gap Shelter is mapped a short way from the pin. The USGS public-land map shows this as State Game Land 211. Wear orange in hunting season.',[W('Rausch Gap, Pennsylvania')]),
N('abandoned-pa-turnpike','Abandoned Pennsylvania Turnpike','Wells Township','pac','landmark','standing',40.04849,-78.12908,'site','ask','hike','','1940–1968',
  'A stretch of the original 1940 turnpike, built on the grade of the never-finished South Pennsylvania Railroad. Its two-lane Sideling Hill and Rays Hill tunnels caused such congestion that a new alignment replaced it in 1968. The Turnpike Commission sold most of it to the Southern Alleghenies Conservancy for one dollar in 2001, and a joint Bedford and Fulton county authority formed in 2018 later bought it.',
  'The abandoned roadway and both tunnels. The pin is the Sideling Hill Tunnel; the Rays Hill Tunnel is about four miles west.',
  'A four-lane highway and two tunnels left to the woods.',
  'Now officially The Old PA Pike Trail, owned by the Bedford Fulton Joint Recreation Authority. Whether it is formally open is not settled by what was read: the USGS public-land map marks it closed to the public. Confirm with the authority before you go, and carry a light for the tunnels.',[W('Abandoned Pennsylvania Turnpike')],conf='mixed'),
N('scotia-barrens','Scotia','Patton Township','pac','town','traces',40.79978,-77.94611,'area','public','walk','','1880–1911',
  'Andrew Carnegie leased ore land in these pine barrens and in 1880 bought tracts and paid for company houses, a store, a church, stables and an ore washer. About 400 people lived here at the peak. Carnegie sold the works in 1899 and the mines closed in 1911. The federal government spent $500,000 reopening them around the Second World War, found the ore poor, and shut them again.',
  'Not confirmed: the source describes the town and the mines but not what survives on the ground.',
  '','The barrens are State Game Lands 176, which has paths for walking, biking and riding and a shooting range. The pin itself falls on a local preserve on the USGS public-land map, beside the game lands. Wear orange in hunting season.',[W('Scotia, Pennsylvania')],conf='mixed'),
N('masten-pa','Masten','McNett Township','pac','town','ruins',41.5073,-76.82051,'site','public','walk','','1905–1941',
  'A lumber company town founded in 1905. The mill cut its last log on September 18, 1930. A Civilian Conservation Corps camp opened on the site on May 6, 1933 and pulled down some of the remaining buildings; it closed in 1940, and the last family left in 1941.',
  'Some building foundations and a smokestack. The old logging railroad grades now carry the Old Loggers Path.',
  '','The trailhead for the Old Loggers Path loop. The USGS public-land map shows the site in Loyalsock State Forest.',[W('Masten, Pennsylvania')]),
N('kinzua-bridge','Kinzua Bridge','Hamlin Township','paw','rail','ruins',41.76111,-78.58861,'site','public','walk','','1882–2003',
  'A railroad viaduct first built of wrought iron in 94 working days in 1882, when it was the tallest railroad bridge in the world. It was rebuilt in steel in 1900. The state bought it in 1963 as the centrepiece of a park. Restoration began in 2002, but a tornado in July 2003 brought down most of the structure; corroded anchor bolts were part of the cause.',
  'The fallen towers in the valley and the surviving end of the bridge.',
  'The tallest railroad bridge in the world, thrown down by a tornado.',
  'Kinzua Bridge State Park.',[W('Kinzua Bridge')]),
N('camp-michaux','Camp Michaux','Cooke Township','pac','inst','ruins',40.03715,-77.33923,'site','public','walk','','1943–1945',
  'A farm called Bunker Hill became a Civilian Conservation Corps camp, and from 1943 to 1945 a secret camp where prisoners of war were interrogated. Afterwards two church bodies ran it as a youth camp called Camp Michaux.',
  'Ruins of the camp along Michaux Road. The Cumberland County Historical Society publishes a guide to a self-guided walk.',
  'A secret wartime interrogation camp in a state forest.',
  'Michaux State Forest.',[W('Pine Grove Furnace Prisoner of War Interrogation Camp')]),
N('pithole','Pithole','Cornplanter Township','paw','town','traces',41.52089,-79.58699,'area','public','walk','','1865–1877',
  'An oil boom town. Wells struck in January 1865, the town was laid out that May, and by December it was incorporated with about 20,000 people, a 1,100-seat theatre and the world\'s first oil pipeline. Fires and richer strikes elsewhere emptied it, and it was unincorporated by 1877. In 1879 what was left was sold back to the county for $4.37.',
  'A few foundations, and mowed paths that mark the streets and the buildings.',
  'Twenty thousand people to nothing in twelve years.',
  'A Pennsylvania Historical and Museum Commission site since 1961. The visitor center is open from Memorial Day weekend through the summer.',[W('Pithole, Pennsylvania')]),
N('concrete-city','Concrete City','Nanticoke','pane','town','ruins',41.18917,-75.97611,'site','ask','walk','','1911–1924',
  'Company housing built in 1911 for chosen employees of the Delaware, Lackawanna and Western Railroad\'s coal division, and an early American example of the International Style. Its later owner, the Glen Alden Coal Company, would not pay for required improvements and could not knock it down, because it was built too solidly, so it walked away in 1924. The state declared it a historic site in 1998 and dropped a later demolition plan over the cost.',
  'The concrete houses still stand as shells. Fire, police and military units train here, and paintball players use it.',
  'Houses too strong to demolish.',
  'The source does not say who owns it. Treat it as private until you have confirmed otherwise.',[W('Concrete City')],conf='mixed'),
# ---- Maine ----
N('katahdin-iron-works','Katahdin Iron Works','Katahdin Iron Works Township','men','industry','standing',45.44444,-69.175,'site','public','walk','','1845–1890',
  'An ironworks in the Maine woods that ran from 1845 to 1890 on ore identified in 1843. Masons built a 55-foot stone blast furnace and eighteen beehive kilns to make its charcoal. The settlement had a railroad and a 100-room hotel.',
  'The restored blast furnace, one restored charcoal kiln, and some foundations. Nothing else of the mill or the village is left.',
  '','A Maine state historic site in an unorganized township. The state land also takes in Gulf Hagas. Check road access before you go.',[W('Katahdin Iron Works')]),
N('eagle-lake-locomotives','Eagle Lake ghost trains','Northwest Piscataquis','men','rail','ruins',46.32252,-69.37499,'site','public','hike','','1927–',
  'A pulpwood railroad built to carry logs from the Allagash drainage to the Penobscot for the Great Northern Paper mill at Millinocket. It began running on August 1, 1927 and ran for only a few years. The place was so remote that the two steam locomotives were never scrapped. Their engine house burned in 1969, taking the wooden cab of engine number 1.',
  'Two steam locomotives standing in the woods at the site of the Eagle Lake Tramway, the only locomotives for more than 100 miles in any direction.',
  'Two steam engines abandoned where they stopped.',
  'Deep in the Allagash country, reached by logging roads and a trail. Plan the trip with current road and gate information.',[W('Eagle Lake and West Branch Railroad')]),
N('fort-baldwin','Fort Baldwin','Phippsburg','mec','fort','ruins',43.75,-69.785,'area','public','walk','forts','1905–1924',
  'A coast defence fort built between 1905 and 1912 near the mouth of the Kennebec, with three gun batteries. It was disarmed in 1924 and sold to the state. The Army came back early in the Second World War, added four round concrete gun mounts, and in 1943 built a fire control tower that could radio ship positions to the big guns in Casco Bay.',
  'The three concrete batteries and the 1943 fire control tower, in the woods on the hill.',
  '','Fort Baldwin State Historic Site.',[W('Fort Baldwin')]),
N('agamenticus-big-a','The Big A ski area, Mount Agamenticus','York','mes','amuse','traces',43.22311,-70.69216,'area','public','walk','','',
  'A ski area on the north-east side of the mountain, known as the Big A. It had a 2,400-foot double chairlift, a 1,200-foot T-bar and a rope tow, with snowmaking, night skiing and a summit lodge. The source read does not give the years it ran.',
  'Remnants of the ski area near the summit and along the trails.',
  '','A park reservation around the summit.',[W('Mount Agamenticus')],conf='mixed'),
N('battery-steele','Battery Steele','Portland','mes','fort','ruins',43.65889,-70.18056,'site','public','walk','forts','1942',
  'A gun battery finished in 1942 on the ocean side of Peaks Island, armed with two 16-inch guns. It was among the largest batteries ever built in the United States. After decades unused, islanders formed the Peaks Island Land Preserve in 1995 to buy the land and keep it open.',
  'The long concrete battery with its two gun positions.',
  '','Peaks Island Land Preserve land, kept as a public space. Peaks Island is reached by boat.',[W('Battery Steele')]),
]
